<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · 1561 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Old 100th</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi>Old Hundredth</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A tune from the Protestant psalm book of Geneva, sung since 1561 to the English words of Psalm 100: all people on earth, sing to God.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>William Kethe (&quot;All People That on Earth Do Dwell&quot;, Psalm 100); also sung to Thomas Ken's Doxology (1674)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>attributed to Louis Bourgeois</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Reformed Protestants of Geneva and the English and Scottish exiles there</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, French</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Geneva, Switzerland</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>tune in the second edition of the Genevan Psalter; English words 1561</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (tune from the Genevan Psalter (16th century); words of Psalm 100 by William Kethe (1561))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Old Hundredth&quot;, or &quot;Old Hundred&quot;, is a hymn tune in long metre from the second edition of the Genevan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-psalms">Psalter</a>, usually attributed to the French composer Louis Bourgeois (c. 1510 – c. 1560). The psalter was compiled over a number of years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, in answer to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a>'s teaching that the communal singing of psalms in the people's own language is a foundation of church life; in the Catholic practice of the time the sacred texts were chanted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> by the clergy alone. Bourgeois and other Calvinist musicians supplied new melodies and adapted others from sacred and secular sources. Calvin meant them to be sung in unison in church, with harmonised versions for singing at home, and the psalter was completed in 1562.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> the tune was set to a French paraphrase of Psalm 134. It takes its English name from the 100th Psalm, in the version &quot;All People That on Earth Do Dwell&quot; attributed to the Scottish clergyman William Kethe, who was in exile at Geneva while the Scottish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> was only beginning; it first appeared in the Anglo-Genevan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-psalms">Psalter</a> of 1561. Any hymn in long metre, four lines of eight syllables, fits the tune. The Doxology, &quot;Praise God, from Whom All Blessings Flow&quot;, written in 1674 by Thomas Ken, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, as the last verse of his &quot;Awake, My Soul, and With the Sun&quot;, is the text most often sung to it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where it is especially linked with the Mainline Protestant churches. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-watts">Isaac Watts</a>'s paraphrase of Psalm 117 is sung to it, shape-note singers of the Sacred Harp use it for Tate and Brady's Psalm 95, and Hiram Bingham I translated a Hawaiian version, Hoʻonani i ka <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makua">Makua</a> mau.</p><p>It also carries German Lutheran chorales, among them Paul Eber's &quot;Herr Gott, dich loben alle wir&quot;, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philipp Melanchthon</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> used as the cantus firmus of his chorale cantata BWV 130. A Voluntary on the Old Hundredth for organ is ascribed to John Blow or to his pupil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-purcell">Henry Purcell</a>. Ralph Vaughan Williams arranged Kethe's version, with a brass fanfare and a 1621 setting by John Dowland, for the coronation of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> in 1953, where it was sung.</p><p>The tune runs through later music and writing: Hubert Parry's Three Chorale Fantasias, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-mendelssohn">Felix Mendelssohn</a>'s Piano Trio in C minor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> Thomson's score for The Plow that Broke the Plains and Paul Hindemith's Trauermusik (both 1936), and Benjamin Britten's cantata St Nicolas (1948). In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-twain">Mark Twain</a>'s The Adventures of Tom Sawyer the congregation sings &quot;Old Hundred&quot; when the boys believed dead appear at their own funeral. It was the first piece transmitted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-graham-bell">Alexander Graham Bell</a>'s first demonstration before the American Academy of Arts and Sciences in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> on 10 May 1876.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>Kethe's words are a metrical paraphrase of Psalm 100, which calls on all people on earth to praise God; the Doxology sung to the same tune is a single verse of praise. The tune is plain and stately, in long metre (four lines of eight syllables), made for a whole congregation to sing together.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>All people that on earth do dwell,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Sing to the Lord with cheerful voice;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Him serve with mirth, His praise forth tell;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Come ye before Him and rejoice.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Praise God, from Whom all blessings flow;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Praise Him, all creatures here below;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Praise Him above, ye heavenly host;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Praise Father, Son, and Holy Ghost.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: William Kethe, Psalm 100, Anglo-Genevan Psalter (1561), first verse; as quoted in Wikipedia's article (English Wikipedia). Text from Wikipedia (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_100th" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Old 100th</a>); public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="TiX3kVxm5Ks" data-title="Old 100th: the Choir of King's College, Cambridge, sing All People That on Earth Do Dwell in Vaughan Williams's arrangement for choir, brass and organ"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">the Choir of King's College, Cambridge, sing All People That on Earth Do Dwell in Vaughan Williams's arrangement for choir, brass and organ</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel The Choir of King’s College, Cambridge - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TiX3kVxm5Ks" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Herr Gott, dich loben alle wir, BWV 130 (Johann Sebastian Bach)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the tune as cantus firmus</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Voluntary on the Old Hundredth (John Blow or Henry Purcell)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">organ piece on the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Three Chorale Fantasias (Hubert Parry)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">based on the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Plow that Broke the Plains (Virgil Thomson)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1936</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in a film score</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Trauermusik (Paul Hindemith)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1936</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">St Nicolas (Benjamin Britten)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1948</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in a cantata</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Piano Trio in C minor, Op. 66 (Felix Mendelssohn)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">in the finale</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Old 100th Psalm Tune (Ralph Vaughan Williams)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1953</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement for the coronation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Volunteer Organist (George L. Spaulding)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1893</span><span class="sg-chain-h">played in the refrain</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Adventures of Tom Sawyer (Mark Twain)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung in a novel</span></li></ul></section>
